obsolescence approaching economic end-of-life. At $168,390 market value with active homestead exemption, the improvement-to-land ratio suggests the structure contributes roughly $80K-$90K while the underlying lot holds $78K-$88K in residual value. Payment history reveals ownership flux: JUAN GONZALEZ paid 2025, JUAN F GONZALEZ paid 2024, but JOSELYN HERNANDEZ also made 2024 payments—indicating possible spousal contribution or intra-family transfer complications. The fact that four defendants are named (Juan Fernando Gonzalez and Graciela Janet Hernandez at-property, plus Micaela and Jose Acosta at 630 Delaware as in-rem-only) suggests potential inheritance or co-ownership dispute. The Acostas'' in-rem status with different address points to prior owner heirs who retained legal interest but abandoned occupancy. This fractured title scenario creates negotiation leverage but complicates clean acquisition.\n\n**FINANCIAL PICTURE:**\nFiled at $18,013.35 on July 6, 2026, the live balance now stands at $18,346.13—a modest $332.78 accrual over approximately 60 days, indicating penalty/interest accumulation of ~$1,998 annually (11.1% effective rate). DISD carries the heaviest burden at $8,381.75 (46.5% of total), followed by City of Dallas at $5,399.43 (30%). Current debt-to-value ratio sits at **10.9%**—relatively low, meaning equity position remains strong at ~$150K. True payoff today requires $18,346.13 plus Linebarger''s attorney fees (typically 15-20% of tax debt, or $2,750-$3,670), plus court costs (~$400-$600), totaling **$21,500-$22,600 all-in**. Penalty acceleration adds approximately $166/month moving forward. The active homestead exemption confirms owner-occupancy, reducing likelihood of voluntary walk-away but increasing vulnerability if the Gonzalez household faces income disruption. With $150K equity cushion, this isn''t a desperation distress—it''s cash-flow distress with asset protection','\n',char(10)),NULL,'pre_judgment',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-07T12:4