10 market value but shows 50% depreciation after 78 years—we''re looking at a property approaching tear-down economics. The land-to-improvement ratio isn''t explicitly stated, but with Lancaster lot values and this level of depreciation, I''m estimating $50-65K in land value against $130-145K in dated structure value. Critical red flags: Martha Rivers holds 100% ownership per DCAD (deed transfer 6/8/2000), yet the lawsuit names Edward Brown Sr., Eva Mae Brown, and John Will Brown alongside her with "Address Unknown" for three of four defendants. This screams **intestate succession or clouded title**. Payment history tells the real story: Rivers paid 2022 herself, Texas Tax Solution II LLC (a tax relief outfit) paid 2019, then complete radio silence for three years. She''s either incapacitated, deceased without probate, or financially collapsed. The Browns are likely heirs who don''t know they''re connected to this asset or don''t care. No homestead exemption confirms absentee/non-owner-occupied status—this property generates zero emotional attachment or utility for any party involved.\n\n## FINANCIAL PICTURE\n\nFiling amount shows $18,674.05 (July 2026), but **live DCAD balance has already climbed to $19,175.83**—that''s $501.78 in penalty acceleration over approximately 8-9 months, tracking at roughly $55-60/month in compounding penalties and interest. The debt-to-value ratio sits at 9.8% ($19,175.83 ÷ $194,810), which is manageable but accelerating. Breaking down the tax stack: Dallas ISD represents 44.5% of the burden ($8,305.48), City of Dallas takes 31.7% ($5,912.57), and Dallas County/Parkland/College split the remainder. With penalties already representing 51.2% of base taxes ($9,322.52 in penalties vs $12,351.53 in base tax), we''re past the inflection point where costs compound aggressively. **True payoff today requires $19,175.83 plus approximately $2,500-3,000 in legal fees/court costs if Linebarger hasn','\n',char(10)),NULL,'pre_judgment',NULL,NULL,NULL,